Component Graph\n40\t\n41\t| Strategy component | Canonical Jira component | Owner team (from architecture context) | Classification |\n42\t|---|---|---|---|\n43\t| RHOAI web console | Dashboard | RHOAI Dashboard | **Active** — report views, data sourcing, CSV export |\n44\t| Notebook controller | Notebooks | Workbenches | **Passive** — already tracks last-activity timestamps; no code changes (\"the data exists\") |\n45\t| Kubeflow Pipelines | Data Science Pipelines | RHOAI Pipelines | **Passive** — already retains run completion metadata; no code changes |\n46\t| (HLR-4 documentation) | Documentation | RHOAI Documentation | Active — substantive docs authoring |\n47\t\n48\tComponent name mappings (`.context/rhai-components.txt`): \"RHOAI web console\"\n49\t→ `Dashboard`; \"Notebook controller\" → `Notebooks`; \"Kubeflow Pipelines\" →\n50\t`Data Science Pipelines`. All three plus `Documentation` are canonical names.\n51\t\n52\t**Passive-component handling:** the strategy's premise is that the notebook\n53\tcontroller and Kubeflow Pipelines already hold the needed data and need no\n54\tchanges — only the console surfaces it. Validation that the reports correctly\n55\treflect each passive component's data therefore becomes acceptance criteria on\n56\tthe nearest active component's epic (E001), not separate epics. The\n57\tdifferent-team exception does not fire: no team involvement is stated in the\n58\tstrategy, and the validation is a data-reconciliation check performed by the\n59\tconsole team, not a sign-off owned by the Workbenches or RHOAI Pipelines\n60\tteams.\n61\t\n62\t**New component detection:** all three code components have\n63\tarchitecture-context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/dashboard.md`,\n64\t`notebooks.md`, `data-science-pipelines.md`) — all in-platform, all Konflux\n65\tonboarded. No onboarding chain is needed. `Documentation` has no\n66\tarchitecture-context file, but it is a work-product component routed through\n67\tthe Red Hat docs content process (`docs-authoring`), not a platform component\n68\tto provision — no repo/konflux onboarding applies to it.\n69\t\n70\t## Step 3 — Investigation Epics\n71\t\n72\tThe strategy states \"Open Questions: None\", has no risks table, no\n73\tassumptions, no pending reviews, and no conditional ADRs. A systematic scan\n74\tfound one implicit assumption — that the notebook controller's last-activity\n75\ttimestamps and KFP run metadata are reachable by the console within its\n76\texisting interfaces (architecture context: the Dashboard uses the Kubernetes\n77\tAPI and component CRs/APIs only). The strategy explicitly asserts the data\n78\texists and is sourceable, and the resolution (where exactly the timestamp is\n79\tread from) would not change which epics exist — worst case it widens the\n80\tsourcing work inside the same console epic. Per the\n81\timplementation-detail/genuine-unknown distinction this is a validation\n82\tassumption: captured as an acceptance criterion on E001 and flagged as a\n83\tscope-trap health warning below. **No Investigation epics.**\n84\t\n85\t## Step 4 — HLR to Epic Mapping\n86\t\n87\t- HLR-1 (P1) and HLR-2 (P1) both land in the Dashboard console as one\n88\t  logical change: a per-project idle/stale resource reporting surface. They\n89\t  share the same console section, project-scoped navigation, table components,\n90\t  and export path — splitting them would duplicate the reporting surface\n91\t  scaffolding. Single epic (E001).\n92\t- HLR-3 (P2, CSV export) is **bundled** into E001: it is a presentation\n93\t  feature over the exact report data structures and UI surfaces built for\n94\t  HLR-1/HLR-2 — not independently schedulable without duplicating them.\n95\t- HLR-4 (P2, documentation) is a distinct work-product stream → separate\n96\t  `docs-authoring` epic (E002). Per the docs-authoring priority exception,\n97\t  its priority derives from the strategy frontmatter `priority: Normal` →\n98\t  P2 (not inherited from E001's P1).\n99\t\n100\t## Epic List\n101\t\n102\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n103\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n104\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle and stale resource reports in the web console | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n105\t| RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Document the idle/stale resource reports and safe reclamation | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Documentation | P2 |\n106\t\n107\t## Dependency DAG\n108\t\n109\t```mermaid\n110\tgraph TD\n111\t    E001[RHAISTRAT-9016-E001: Idle and stale resource reports]\n112\t    E002[RHAISTRAT-9016-E002: Documentation for the reports]\n113\t    E001 --> E002\n114\t```\n115\t\n116\t## DAG Justification\n117\t\n118\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n119\t|---|---|---|\n120\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring blocked by ALL Implementation epics) | Documentation describes the reports that E001 builds; it must reflect what was actually implemented, including the configurable stale-run age and export behavior. |\n121\t\n122\tNo other edges: E001 is the only code epic (no intra-component splits — one\n123\tlogical change, one team, one repo). The Dashboard is already Konflux\n124\tonboarded, so no onboarding epics or Rule 25 build-pipeline ACs apply.\n125\t\n126\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n127\t\n128\t| HLR | Priority | Epic | Notes |\n129\t|---|---|---|---|\n130\t| HLR-1 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Idle-workbench report (last activity, owner, reserved resources) |\n131\t| HLR-2 | P1 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | Stale pipeline-run report (configurable age, retained artifacts) |\n132\t| HLR-3 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E001 | (bundled) CSV export shares the reports' data structures and UI surface — not separately schedulable without duplicating them |\n133\t| HLR-4 | P2 | RHAISTRAT-9016-E002 | Substantive docs authoring; distinct work stream and component |\n134\t\n135\tAll four HLRs are covered; none orphaned.\n136\t\n137\t## Health Warnings\n138\t\n139\t- **Missing strategy sections (non-blocking):** the strategy has no Impacted\n140\t  Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria,\n141\t  and no NFRs. Team assignments were taken from architecture-context files;\n142\t  epic ACs were derived from the HLR text. Owners should confirm team\n143\t  assignments at planning time.\n144\t- **Scope trap — data accessibility assumption:** the strategy asserts the\n145\t  notebook controller's last-activity timestamps are available to be\n146\t  surfaced. Architecture context says activity is tracked per kernel through\n147\t  Jupyter server API probes and that the Dashboard accesses components via\n148\t  the Kubernetes API only. If the timestamps turn out not to be readable\n149\t  through the console's existing interfaces, a follow-up epic in the\n150\t  `Notebooks` component (surfacing the data on an interface the console can\n151\t  read) would be required. Mitigated as a verification AC on E001; flagged\n152\t  here for human verification during refinement of E001.\n153\t- **No priority inversions detected:** both P1 HLRs map to one P1 epic; P2\n154\t  items are correctly bundled (HLR-3, shared surface) or separated (HLR-4,\n155\t  docs-authoring exception).\n156\t\n157\t## Tiered Delivery\n158\t\n159\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing; all work is\n160\tin-platform.\n161\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-a1dbeac2ed95674f","agent_id":"aa9d08f0c8c5b4765","session_id":"a9a305b9-00c8-468f-a658-3bf7862f04ab","uuid":"0c346166-0f4d-4c67-b422-56902e782402","timestamp":"2026-10-26T14:15:24.934Z","tool_result_meta":[{"id":"chatcmpl-tool-bd65380e65a59d39","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}],"subagent_type":"claude","task_description":"Review RHAISTRAT-9016 decomposition"}
